  "limitations": [
    "Monetary costs involved in purchasing the VR equipment (p. 9)",
    "Requirement for highly trained and knowledgeable experts to develop and operate the digital interactive environments (p. 11)",
    "Current VR prototype's 3D models are not exchangeable or editable (p. 12)",
    "Need for more advanced user interfaces to allow users to upload their own 3D models (p. 12)",
    "Limited range of integrated sensory experiences (currently four senses) (p. 11)",
    "Quality of student-created 3D modelling may have affected perceived consistency between VR and real world (p. 9)",
    "Abstraction in site model construction due to time limits (p. 11)",
    "Limited size of the tested population prevented statistical difference or correlational studies comparing sub-groups (p. 8)"
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study evaluates a multi-sensory VR prototype designed to support placemaking through immersive environments. The core claim is that immersive mixed-media environments can effectively engage users with intangible sensory and temporal aspects of place for design evaluation. Key findings indicate that participants rated the VR approach as highly effective, engaging, and relatively easy to use, with 3D models, 360 videos, and soundscapes being the most important components. Users reported a strong sense of presence but also noted divided opinions on the consistency between VR and the real world, and desired additional features like weather, simulated people, and smells. Limitations include the high cost of equipment, the need for expert developers, current prototype's lack of editable 3D models, and the limited range of sensory experiences integrated.",
